  "design_notes": "Quota reads as a low, wide wall: a 2.7 m hazard-striped blade out front, a broad slab body planted between two heavy treads, and a squat cab head that barely rises above its shoulders, so in silhouette it is pure forward pressure. Every shape is a straight line or a right angle, rigid piston-bar arms, a flat tilted blade, a single long horizontal eye-slit, because Quota's logic only knows the shortest path from here to done. The one circle on its body is the white stopwatch dial set dead-centre in its chest, making time literally the heart of the machine and the first thing the audience reads. Safety yellow paint worn to dark steel at the edges says it has worked hard and proudly for years, the black hazard stripes on blade, visor and shoulders mark it as a danger to anything in its way, and the amber eye and roof beacon tie it to the pulsing warning lights of the Pipeworks until those same ambers warm into the gold sunrise over the Grove."
}